<!DOCTYPE qhelp PUBLIC
  "-//Semmle//qhelp//EN"
  "qhelp.dtd">

<qhelp>

  <overview>
    <p>
      An attacker who can influence a downloaded executable, installer, module, assembly, or script
      can execute code when that artifact is run or loaded without effective verification.
      This query requires both an insecure or explicitly modeled remotely controlled origin and
      a downstream code-execution or code-loading operation.
    </p>
    <p>
      Plain HTTP downloads are analyzed under an on-path-attacker threat model. Remote-input
      models can also identify downloads whose entire URL comes from an inbound request.
      Ordinary function parameters, environment variables, and generic HTTPS download responses
      are not automatically considered attacker controlled.
    </p>
    <p>
      Fixed HTTPS origins, download-only and extraction-only operations, and ordinary configuration
      retrieval are outside this query's strict scope. These exclusions are not proof that the
      corresponding operations are safe. Hostname substrings and build, test, or dependency paths
      are not used to establish severity or trust.
    </p>
    <p>
      The query tracks modeled file copies, moves, renames, file reads and writes, and archive members
      constructed below an extraction directory. File lifecycle and verification paths are evaluated
      within a control-flow scope; custom filesystem wrappers and runtime-computed paths may not be
      resolved. Findings identify high-impact candidates, not a guarantee of high severity in every
      deployment.
    </p>
  </overview>

  <recommendation>
    <p>
      Verify the integrity of every downloaded artifact before using it. Compute a cryptographic hash
      of the downloaded file with <code>Get-FileHash</code> and compare it against a known-good hash
      that is obtained through a trusted, out-of-band channel (for example, the signed release notes
      or a published checksum file). Only use the artifact when the computed hash matches the expected
      value; otherwise, discard it and fail. Where available, prefer verifying a digital signature of
      the artifact in addition to, or instead of, a plain hash comparison.
    </p>
    <p>
      Computing or logging a hash is not an integrity check by itself. Compare the complete digest
      against a trusted expected value, and ensure that a mismatch stops execution before the artifact
      is installed, loaded, extracted, or run. An optional check, a swallowed exception, or a check
      performed only after the artifact has been used does not provide this protection.
    </p>
    <p>
      Signature verification must also establish the intended publisher. For Authenticode, check
      that the signature is valid and that the signer certificate matches a trusted pinned
      certificate thumbprint. Modeled native checks include GPG with a fixed
      <code>--assert-signer</code> fingerprint and Cosign blob verification with fixed certificate
      identity and OIDC issuer. The native verifier's successful exit code must be enforced before
      use; merely invoking a signature tool or inspecting its output does not verify the artifact.
      A constant path to a public-key file alone does not establish that the key is trusted.
    </p>
  </recommendation>

  <example>
    <p>
      In the following example, an executable is downloaded over HTTP and then run directly.
      An on-path attacker can replace the executable, which is never verified.
    </p>
    <sample src="examples/powershell/DownloadWithoutIntegrityCheckBad.ps1" />

    <p>
      In the following example, the downloaded artifact's SHA-256 hash is compared against the expected
      value before it is used. The artifact is removed and an error is raised if the integrity check
      fails, so a tampered download is never executed.
    </p>
    <sample src="examples/powershell/DownloadWithoutIntegrityCheckGood.ps1" />
  </example>

  <references>
    <li>
      Common Weakness Enumeration:
      <a href="https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/494.html">CWE-494: Download of Code Without Integrity Check</a>.
    </li>
    <li>
      Common Weakness Enumeration:
      <a href="https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/829.html">CWE-829: Inclusion of Functionality from Untrusted Control Sphere</a>.
    </li>
    <li>
      Microsoft Learn:
      <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/powershell/module/microsoft.powershell.utility/get-filehash">Get-FileHash</a>.
    </li>
    <li>
      GnuPG:
      <a href="https://gnupg.org/documentation/manuals/gnupg/GPG-Configuration-Options.html">GPG configuration options</a>.
    </li>
    <li>
      Sigstore:
      <a href="https://docs.sigstore.dev/cosign/verifying/verify/">Verifying signatures</a>.
    </li>
  </references>

</qhelp>
